{"text": "Daniel learns most successfully through curriculum compacting after demonstrated mastery; acceleration in selected topics; long-term inquiry with authentic audience; and discussion with similar-readiness peers. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Daniel may struggle with repetitive practice after mastery; fear of making visible mistakes; group tasks without meaningful challenge; and uneven executive skills on routine work. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-P-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Daniel", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.5125, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 6}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.525, "end": 0.925, "start_char": 7, "end_char": 13}, {"text": "most", "start": 0.9375, "end": 1.2125, "start_char": 14, "end_char": 18}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.225, "end": 1.9375, "start_char": 19, "end_char": 31}, {"text": "through", "start": 1.95, "end": 2.175, "start_char": 32, "end_char": 39}, {"text": "curriculum", "start": 2.1875, "end": 2.725, "start_char": 40, "end_char": 50}, {"text": "compacting", "start": 2.7375, "end": 3.4375, "start_char": 51, "end_char": 61}, {"text": "after", "start": 3.45, "end": 3.725, "start_char": 62, "end_char": 67}, {"text": "dem